        "geology": "­- The predominant forms are sands, clay marls, clays and crushed rock aggregates.\r\n- ­Floodplain areas and colluvial deposits occur along rivers.\r\n- Occasionally, volcanic rock formations such as basaltic tufa and vulcanic tufa (e.g. Kapfensteiner Kogel) as well as trachyte blocks with eruption breccia and tufa (e.g. Gleichenberger Kogel) are encountered.",
        "hydrology": "The Raba (Raab) is the main river of the region. Mean annual aeral precipitation and variability (1961-1990):\r\n - Mean annual aeral precipitation using water balance data: 700 mm - 850 mm\r\n - Minimum annual precipitation depth: 550 mm - 700 mm\r\n - Range of annual precipitation depth: 400 mm - 550 mm\r\n - Coefficient of variation of annual precipitation depth: 12% - 16%\r\n - Percentage of summer precipitation in mean annual precipitation: 65% - 70%\r\n\r\nExtreme multi-day precipitation (1961-1990):\r\n - Maximum precipitation (2 days): 100 mm - 125 mm\r\n - Maximum precipitation (4 days): 125 mm - 150 mm\r\n - Maximum precipitation (6 days): 150 mm - 200 mm\r\n\r\nDuration of dry spells (1971-1999):\r\n - Mean duration of dry spells (Threshold 0.2 mm): 4 days - 5 days\r\n - Mean maximum duration of dry spells (Threshold 0.2 mm): 22 days - 26 days\r\n\r\nEvapotranspiration and runoff (1961-1990):\r\n - Mean annual potential evapotranspiration: 625 mm - 650 mm\r\n - Mean annual aeral actual evapotranspiration using water balance data: 550 mm - 600 mm\r\n - Mean annual depth of runoff using water balance data: 0mm - 250 mm\r\n\r\nSeasonal water balance (1961-1990):\r\n - Change of water storage during autumn (Sep-Nov): (25 mm) - (50 mm)\r\n - Change of water storage during spring (Mar-May): (-50 mm) - (-75 mm)\r\n - Change of water storage during winter (Dec-Feb): (25 mm) - (50 mm)\r\n - Change of water storage during summer (Jun-Aug): (-25 mm) - (25 mm)",
        "soils": "- Along main rivers, lime-free brown fluvisols occur with increasing characteristics of gley soils.\r\n- Gley soils appear on alluvial fan-shaped deposits and on gently sloping terrain. Occasionally, characteristics of gley soils are particularly pronounced on older terrace remnants at higher levels (stagnic gleysols).\r\n- A high degree of soil diversity emerges along the upper slopes and hill top regions. In particular, this soil types include large areas of eutric cambisols, less developed raw soils as well as relic soils on rare occasions.",
        "vegetation": "- Arable land has been dominated by maize and wheat, by other crop such as wine and pumpkin to a lesser extent.\r\n- Grassland and mixed orchard meadows with varieties of apple, pear and peach.\r\n- Woodland is characterized by deciduous forest regions, particularly with oak, hornbeam, beech and sweet chestnut as well as pine and spruce."
      },
